# Line endings are decided HERE, by the repository, not by whatever
# `core.autocrlf` happens to be set to on a contributor's machine.
#
# Why this file exists: rewriting a tracked file from a script on Windows -
# `Path.write_text()`, `Set-Content` - rewrites EVERY line ending in it, not
# just the edited lines, and `git diff` cannot show you that. Until this file
# existed, the only thing keeping that churn out of the repository was one
# machine's `core.autocrlf=true`. A clone without it would have committed the
# lot on the first scripted edit.
#
# `eol=lf` covers the working tree as well as the stored blob, deliberately:
# when the checked-out bytes match the committed bytes, a stray CRLF rewrite
# shows up immediately as a diff instead of hiding behind a clean filter.
* text=auto eol=lf

# One file opts itself out and it is not a mistake: `tests/test_account_manager.ps1`
# carries a lone CR - a 0x0D with no 0x0A after it - inside a regex character class
# written with literal control characters. A lone CR is one of the things git's
# `text=auto` detection treats as binary, so the rule below never reaches that file
# and it keeps CRLF in both the index and the working tree, stably and without churn
# (`git ls-files --eol` reports it as `i/-text w/-text`). Forcing `text` on it would
# rewrite the bytes of that regex, so it is left alone deliberately.

# The exception, and the reason it is one: a Windows batch file is read by
# CMD.EXE, which mis-parses LF-only line endings - a `goto` label or a
# multi-line `if` block can silently take the wrong branch. There are none in
# the tree today; this is here so that adding one cannot break it.
*.bat text eol=crlf
*.cmd text eol=crlf
